From 78b94e00ddd518c95577450380aafa0a4eab1764 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:02:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 148/148] hydra + scene: a mix of many standard_surface blended
 by its inputs; SVM stack diagnostics

A terrain mixing ten tiled rock materials by nine mix_surfaceshader could
not compile in Cycles: 120 closures against the kernel's 64, and some 2000
SVM stack slots against 255 (offsets are a byte; the stack cannot grow).
The growth is structural: each layer's tiled coordinates and hex weights
feed its colour, its normal and its roughness, and the Sethi-Ullman order,
optimal for trees, generates the whole colour tree first and keeps every
layer's shared values alive for the other two.

FoldSurfaceMix (mtlx_expand.cpp): a mix tree of four or more
standard_surface becomes one standard_surface whose written inputs are the
mixes of the leaves' by the same weights - the usual material-blend
approximation, normals renormalised - with the mixes chained layer by layer
and each layer's geometry lookups tied to the previous layer's last mix
(value + 1e-30 x it), so the compiler takes one layer at a time: 188 slots
for the ten layers. Three leaves or fewer keep the exact closure mix. The
folded terrain is within 4 % of Karma's closure mix.

The SVM compiler reports each shader's peak stack and word count at info,
and a shader that does not fit goes on allocating on a virtual stack to
report the peak it would need, with the live values by node at the overflow;
Cycles nodes are named after their Hydra node for that. (K-110)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
